+++
title: Book of the Dead
description: Ancient Egyptian funerary text
key-facts:
  - name: What it is
    value: An ancient Egyptian funerary text, generally written on papyrus
  - name: In use
    value: From the beginning of the New Kingdom (around 1550 BC) to around 50 BC
  - name: Egyptian name
    value: rw nw prt m hrw, translated as Spells of Coming Forth by Day
  - name: Spells known
    value: Some 192, though no single manuscript contains them all
  - name: Price
    value: One deben of silver, perhaps half the annual pay of a laborer
  - name: Length
    value: The longest is 40 m long while some are as short as 1 m
image-plan:
  - file: El_pesado_del_corazón_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The scribe Hunefer's heart is weighed on the scale of Maat against
      the feather of truth by the jackal-headed Anubis, while the ibis-headed
      Thoth records the result. If the heart equals exactly the weight of the
      feather, Hunefer passes into the afterlife; if not, the waiting Ammit –
      crocodile, lion and hippopotamus – eats it. From the Papyrus of Hunefer,
      c. 1275 BC.
  - file: Hieroglyph_Text_from_Teti_I_pyramid.jpg
    role: inline
    section: development
    caption: Part of the Pyramid Texts, a precursor of the Book of the Dead,
      inscribed on the tomb of Teti.
  - file: Medjed_Cairo_95658.png
    role: inline
    section: development
    caption: "Deities with varying animal heads, Medjed on the far left: a
      vignette from the papyrus for Ankhefenkhonsu."
  - file: Bookofthedeadspell17.jpg
    role: inline
    section: spells
    caption: The mystical Spell 17, from the Papyrus of Ani. Left to right, the
      vignette shows the god Nu as the primordial ocean; a gateway to the
      realm of Osiris; the Eye of Horus; the celestial cow Mehet-Weret; and a
      human head rising from a coffin, guarded by the four Sons of Horus.
  - file: BOTDSpell7980.jpg
    role: inline
    section: spells
    caption: Spell 79 attaches the soul to the body and Spell 80 prevents
      incoherent speech, here painted on a coffin fragment of c. 747–656 BC.
  - file: Egypt_dauingevekten.jpg
    role: inline
    section: egyptian-concepts-of-death-and-afterlife
    caption: A vignette in the Papyrus of Ani from Spell 30B, Spell For Not
      Letting Ani's Heart Create Opposition Against Him, in the Gods' Domain,
      containing a depiction of the ba of the deceased.
  - file: Libro_dei_morti_di_Kha_AFP_0310.tif
    role: inline
    section: afterlife
    caption: The Book of the Dead of Kha, Papyrus Turin 1791, from the tomb TT8 of
      Kha and Merit. Top left is the Ba bird, the soul of the deceased Kha.
      Museo Egizio, Turin.
  - file: Bookofthedead-144145.jpg
    role: full
    section: afterlife
    caption: Two 'gate spells'. On the top register Ani and his wife face the
      'seven gates of the House of Osiris'; below, they meet ten of the 21
      'mysterious portals of the House of Osiris in the Field of Reeds'. All
      are guarded by unpleasant protectors.
  - file: Ani_chap125.jpg
    role: inline
    section: judgment
    caption: Some of the 42 Judges of Maat, seated and drawn small, in the Papyrus
      of Ani. The deceased had to address each of them by name.
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    role: drop
    section: judgment
    caption: The Weighing of the Heart in the Papyrus of Hunefer (19th Dynasty, c.
      1300 BC). Dropped as the same scene as the opening image.
  - file: PinedjemIIBookOfTheDead-BritishMuseum-August21-08.jpg
    role: inline
    section: producing-a-book-of-the-dead
    caption: Part of the Book of the Dead of Pinedjem II. The text is hieratic,
      except for hieroglyphics in the vignette; the red pigment and the joins
      between papyrus sheets are both visible.
  - file: Book_of_the_Dead_of_the_Goldworker_of_Amun,_Sobekmose,_Mummy_Chamber,_31.1777e.jpg
    role: inline
    section: producing-a-book-of-the-dead
    caption: The Book of the Dead of Sobekmose, the Goldworker of Amun – an owner
      from outside the royal family. Brooklyn Museum, 31.1777e.
  - file: Papyrus_Ani_curs_hiero.jpg
    role: inline
    section: producing-a-book-of-the-dead
    caption: A close-up of the Papyrus of Ani, showing the cursive hieroglyphs of
      the text.
  - file: Carl_Richard_Lepsius_(1810-1884).jpg
    role: inline
    section: discovery-translation-interpretation-and-preservation
    caption: Karl Richard Lepsius, first translator of a complete Book of the Dead
      manuscript, and the man who coined the name in 1842.
further:
  - slug: duat
    why: The underworld the spells were written to get a dead person through, and
      where Osiris was confined.
  - slug: osiris
    why: The god into whose presence the dead were led; the deceased is often
      called 'The Osiris – [Name]'.
  - slug: thoth
    why: Scribe of the gods, who records the result of the weighing of the heart
      and was held to have invented hieroglyphic script.
image: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/El_pesado_del_coraz%C3%B3n_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg
images:
  - file: El_pesado_del_corazón_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/El_pesado_del_coraz%C3%B3n_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg/330px-El_pesado_del_coraz%C3%B3n_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg
    width: 1035
    height: 651
    caption: This detailed scene, from the Papyrus of Hunefer (c. 1275 BC), shows
      the scribe Hunefer's heart being weighed on the scale of Maat against
      the feather of truth, by the jackal-headed Anubis. The ibis-headed
      Thoth, scribe of the gods, records the result. If his heart equals
      exactly the weight of the feather, Hunefer is allowed to pass into the
      afterlife. If not, he is eaten by the waiting chimeric devouring
      creature Ammit composed of the deadly crocodile, lion, and hippopotamus.
      Vignettes such as these were a common illustration in Egyptian books of
      the dead.
  - file: Hieroglyph_Text_from_Teti_I_pyramid.jpg
